# Terminal Command Safety Rule

**MANDATORY: Avoid terminal commands that cause shell parsing issues or hanging prompts.**

## Core Principle

When using the `run_terminal_cmd` tool, ensure commands are simple, safe, and don't trigger shell parsing problems that lead to `dquote>` prompts or hanging processes.

## Prohibited Command Patterns

### ❌ Avoid Complex Quoting

```bash
# DON'T: Complex quotes that cause dquote> prompts
node cli.js --message "Hello world!"
echo "Some string with 'nested quotes'"
timeout 10s command --arg "quoted value"

# DON'T: Commands with unmatched quotes
grep "pattern with missing quote
```

### ❌ Avoid Interactive Commands

```bash
# DON'T: Commands that wait for user input
read -p "Enter value: "
vim filename.txt
nano config.json

# DON'T: Commands that might hang indefinitely
ssh remote-server
telnet hostname port
```

### ❌ Avoid Complex Shell Features

```bash
# DON'T: Complex shell pipelines that might break
command | complex | pipeline | that | might | fail
command $(with nested $(command substitution))

# DON'T: Commands with complex redirections
command > file 2>&1 < input
```

## Preferred Command Patterns

### ✅ Simple Commands Without Quotes

```bash
# GOOD: Simple commands without complex quoting
node --test packages/cli/test/bin.test.ts
ls -la packages/
cat package.json

# GOOD: Use existing test suites instead of manual testing
node --test test-file.ts
npm test
```

### ✅ Basic File Operations

```bash
# GOOD: Simple file operations
ls packages/
mkdir new-directory
rm temp-file.txt
cp source.txt dest.txt

# GOOD: Basic git operations
git status
git add filename
git log --oneline
```

### ✅ Process Information

```bash
# GOOD: Safe information gathering
ps aux | head
which node
node --version
npm --version
```

## Testing Strategy

### ✅ Prefer Test Suites Over Manual CLI Testing

```bash
# GOOD: Use existing test infrastructure
node --test packages/cli/test/bin.test.ts
node --test packages/config/test/config.test.ts

# AVOID: Manual CLI testing with complex arguments
# Instead rely on comprehensive test coverage
```

### ✅ Simple Validation Commands

```bash
# GOOD: Basic functionality checks
node packages/cli/bin.ts help
node packages/cli/bin.ts --version

# AVOID: Complex CLI invocations with quoted arguments
```

## Error Prevention

### ✅ Command Validation Checklist

- [ ] No unmatched quotes or complex quoting
- [ ] No interactive prompts or user input required
- [ ] No commands that might hang indefinitely
- [ ] No complex shell features (pipes, redirections, substitutions)
- [ ] Can be interrupted cleanly with Ctrl+C
- [ ] Has predictable, finite execution time

### ✅ Alternative Approaches

- **Instead of manual CLI testing**: Use existing test suites
- **Instead of complex commands**: Break into simple steps
- **Instead of quoted arguments**: Use test fixtures or configuration files
- **Instead of interactive tools**: Use programmatic alternatives

## Common Violations to Avoid

❌ **Commands with quotes:**

```bash
node cli.js --message "some text"  # Causes dquote> issues
```

❌ **Timeout with complex args:**

```bash
timeout 10s node cli.js --arg "value"  # Shell parsing problems
```

❌ **Interactive commands:**

```bash
read input  # Waits for user input
```

❌ **Complex pipelines:**

```bash
command | grep "pattern" | sort  # Multiple failure points
```

## Implementation Notes

- **Rely on test suites** for validation instead of manual terminal testing
- **Use simple, atomic commands** that don't require complex shell features
- **Verify commands work** in isolation before using in automated contexts
- **Always have exit strategy** - commands should complete or be easily interrupted
- **Test command safety** in a separate terminal before automation

This rule ensures reliable, predictable terminal operations that don't cause hanging prompts, parsing errors, or interactive sessions that can't be automated safely.
